(function(){
  window.AP_LEARNING=window.AP_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visualTitle,visualItems,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,prompt,options)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual:{title:visualTitle,kind:'concept-map',items:visualItems.map(x=>({label:x[0],detail:x[1]}))},mnemonic:{title:mnemonic[0],cue:mnemonic[1],explanation:mnemonic[2]},goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity:{prompt,options:options.map(x=>({text:x[0],correct:x[1],feedback:x[2]}))}});
  window.AP_LEARNING['lesson-10']={version:1,title:'The Nervous System: Introduction, Spinal Cord, and Spinal Nerves',chapter:10,duration:'75–95 minutes',
    outcomes:['Organize the nervous system into central, peripheral, sensory and motor divisions.','Compare neuron structures and the major supporting glial cells.','Explain action-potential conduction and chemical synaptic transmission.','Read gray matter, white matter, tracts, nerves, nuclei and ganglia correctly.','Trace spinal-nerve pathways and a reflex arc.','Preview sympathetic and parasympathetic functions needed by the supplied quiz bank.'],
    modules:[
      {id:'organization',title:'Build the communication map',summary:'The nervous system receives information, integrates it and sends a response.',moments:[
        M('l10-m1','CNS, PNS, sensory and motor','Location and direction create the master map.',[
          'The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) includes nerves and ganglia outside the CNS. Together they form a rapid control and communication network.',
          'Sensory, or afferent, neurons carry information toward the CNS. Motor, or efferent, neurons carry commands from the CNS to muscles and glands. Interneurons inside the CNS connect and process these pathways.'
        ],['CNS = brain + spinal cord.','PNS = nerves + ganglia.','Afferent arrives at the CNS.','Efferent exits the CNS.','Interneurons integrate.'],'Information loop',[['Receptor','Detects change'],['Afferent neuron','Toward CNS'],['Interneuron','Integrates'],['Efferent neuron','Away from CNS'],['Effector','Responds']],['SAME direction','Sensory Afferent; Motor Efferent','SAME pairs pathway role with directional term.'],'A motor neuron is also called efferent; the first neuron from a receptor is sensory/afferent, not motor.','Understanding sensory signals can improve communication about appetite, pain or function without turning a report into a diagnosis.',
        'Which neuron carries a command from spinal cord to skeletal muscle?',[['Motor/efferent',true,'Correct. Efferent pathways exit the CNS.'],['Sensory/afferent',false,'Afferent pathways carry input toward the CNS.'],['Bipolar receptor only',false,'Shape does not replace directional function.']]),
        M('l10-m2','Neuron shapes and parts','Dendrites receive, the soma maintains, and the axon conducts away.',[
          'A neuron has a cell body, dendrites and usually one axon. Multipolar neurons have many dendrites and one axon and are common among motor neurons and interneurons. Most general sensory neurons are unipolar; bipolar neurons occur in special sensory pathways.',
          'Neuron shape and pathway role are related but not identical labels. A neuron may be called sensory because of information direction and unipolar because of structure.'
        ],['Dendrites receive input.','The axon carries signals away from the soma.','Multipolar neurons have many dendrites.','Most general sensory neurons are unipolar.','Bipolar neurons serve special senses.'],'Neuron forms',[['Multipolar','Many dendrites, one axon'],['Unipolar','Single process; common sensory form'],['Bipolar','Two processes; special senses']],['MUB','Multipolar · Unipolar · Bipolar','Picture many, one, and two processes.'],'The soma is metabolically essential; a long axon depends on transport from its cell body.','Neural anatomy can explain why sensory and motor effects differ, but symptom localization belongs to trained clinicians.',
        'Which structural type describes most general sensory neurons?',[['Unipolar',true,'Correct. Most are unipolar in this textbook classification.'],['Multipolar',false,'Most motor neurons and interneurons are multipolar.'],['Acellular',false,'Neurons are cells.']])
      ]},
      {id:'cells-signals',title:'Support and signal',summary:'Glia maintain the neural environment; neurons generate and transmit electrochemical signals.',moments:[
        M('l10-m3','Neuroglia are active partners','Different glia support, defend, insulate and circulate fluid.',[
          'Astrocytes form a supporting network and help regulate the CNS environment. Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the CNS; Schwann cells form myelin in the PNS. Microglia are phagocytic defenders, and ependymal cells line fluid-filled CNS spaces.',
          'Satellite cells support neuron cell bodies in peripheral ganglia. Glia are more numerous than neurons in many regions; the claim that more than 60% of all brain cells are neurons is therefore not a safe generalization.'
        ],['Astrocytes support and regulate.','Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons.','Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons.','Microglia defend.','Ependymal cells line ventricles/central canal.'],'Glial jobs',[['Astrocyte','Support and environment'],['Oligodendrocyte','CNS myelin'],['Schwann cell','PNS myelin'],['Microglia','Defense'],['Ependymal','Fluid-space lining']],['CNS O, PNS S','Oligodendrocyte in the CNS; Schwann in the PNS','Anchor the myelin cells by location.'],'A single oligodendrocyte can myelinate parts of several CNS axons; one Schwann cell wraps one PNS segment.','Adequate overall nutrition supports nervous-system health, but no food can be inferred to repair a specific myelin disorder.',
        'Which cell forms myelin around a peripheral nerve fiber?',[['Schwann cell',true,'Correct. Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons.'],['Astrocyte',false,'Astrocytes support the CNS environment.'],['Microglia',false,'Microglia are immune-like CNS cells.']]),
        M('l10-m4','Action potentials and synapses','Electrical conduction along an axon becomes chemical signaling at many synapses.',[
          'At rest, ion gradients create a membrane potential. A sufficient stimulus opens voltage-gated channels: sodium entry depolarizes the membrane, then potassium movement helps repolarize it. The refractory period supports one-way propagation from the trigger zone along the axon.',
          'Myelin speeds conduction because current effectively jumps between nodes. At a chemical synapse, the action potential triggers neurotransmitter release; transmitter crosses the cleft, binds receptors and changes the next cell. Acetylcholine is a common transmitter, but the body uses many others.'
        ],['Sodium entry drives depolarization.','Potassium movement supports repolarization.','Myelin increases conduction speed.','Neurotransmitters cross chemical synapses.','Refractory behavior supports one-way travel.'],'Signal sequence',[['Rest','Ion gradients'],['Depolarize','Na⁺ entry'],['Repolarize','K⁺ shift'],['Conduct','Along axon'],['Transmit','Neurotransmitter across cleft']],['Na in, K reset','Sodium depolarizes; potassium helps restore','Then transmitter carries the message across the gap.'],'Unmyelinated fibers conduct along the full membrane and are generally slower than comparable myelinated fibers.','Claims about stimulants, depressants or supplements require dose and context; anatomy alone cannot predict individual effects.',
        'What crosses the gap at a chemical synapse?',[['A neurotransmitter',true,'Correct. It binds receptors on the next cell.'],['A sarcolemma',false,'That is a muscle-cell membrane.'],['A tract',false,'A tract is a CNS bundle of axons.']])
      ]},
      {id:'spinal-map',title:'Read the spinal cord and nerves',summary:'Learn the location vocabulary before tracing a pathway.',moments:[
        M('l10-m5','Gray, white, tract, nerve, nucleus, ganglion','The same cellular material receives different names by location.',[
          'Gray matter contains many neuron cell bodies, dendrites and synapses; white matter contains many myelinated axons. In the spinal cord, gray matter forms an inner butterfly-like region surrounded by white columns.',
          'A bundle of axons inside the CNS is a tract; outside it is a nerve. A cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is a nucleus; outside it is a ganglion. These paired terms are frequent test targets.'
        ],['Gray matter emphasizes somas and dendrites.','White matter emphasizes myelinated axons.','CNS axon bundle = tract.','PNS axon bundle = nerve.','PNS cell-body cluster = ganglion.'],'Location vocabulary',[['CNS bundle','Tract'],['PNS bundle','Nerve'],['CNS somas','Nucleus'],['PNS somas','Ganglion']],['TNNG map','Tract/Nucleus in CNS; Nerve/Ganglion in PNS','Sort by axons versus somas, then by location.'],'Ganglion and ganglia refer to the same PNS cell-body structure in singular and plural.',
        'Clear pathway language helps explain why spinal and peripheral problems can produce different patterns without diagnosing the site.',
        'What is a bundle of axons within the CNS called?',[['Tract',true,'Correct. A peripheral bundle would be a nerve.'],['Ganglion',false,'A ganglion is a PNS cluster of cell bodies.'],['Nucleus',false,'A nucleus is a CNS cluster of cell bodies.']]),
        M('l10-m6','Cord, roots and mixed spinal nerves','Sensory and motor fibers separate at roots but combine in each spinal nerve.',[
          'The spinal cord continues from the medulla oblongata through the vertebral canal. Dorsal roots bring sensory information into the cord, and their dorsal-root ganglia contain sensory neuron cell bodies. Ventral roots carry motor output away.',
          'A dorsal and ventral root unite to form a mixed spinal nerve, so every spinal nerve carries both sensory and motor fibers. The epidural space lies between the vertebral canal and the dura mater and contains cushioning tissue and vessels.'
        ],['Cord continues from medulla oblongata.','Dorsal root is sensory.','Ventral root is motor.','All spinal nerves are mixed.','Epidural space lies outside the dura.'],'Root-to-nerve',[['Dorsal root','Sensory input'],['Dorsal-root ganglion','Sensory somas'],['Ventral root','Motor output'],['Spinal nerve','Mixed fibers'],['Epidural space','Outside dura']],['DAVE','Dorsal Afferent; Ventral Efferent','Then the roots unite into a mixed nerve.'],'The adult spinal cord ends above the lowest vertebrae; lower roots descend before exiting.',
        'Spinal pathway knowledge supports safe interdisciplinary communication when function, swallowing or mobility is affected.',
        'Why is a spinal nerve called mixed?',[['It contains both sensory and motor fibers.',true,'Correct. The two root streams combine.'],['It belongs only to the autonomic system.',false,'Spinal nerves carry multiple functional fiber types.'],['It contains gray and white matter.',false,'Those terms describe CNS tissue organization.']])
      ]},
      {id:'responses',title:'Fast responses and autonomic balance',summary:'Reflex arcs protect and regulate; autonomic divisions shift organ activity with context.',moments:[
        M('l10-m7','The reflex arc','A reflex can produce a rapid response before conscious interpretation.',[
          'A basic somatic reflex arc includes receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron and skeletal-muscle effector. Some reflexes are monosynaptic; many use interneurons and multiple synapses.',
          'The brain can become aware of the event and modify later behavior, but the spinal circuitry does not need to wait for a conscious decision. Reflex testing therefore samples both sensory and motor pathway integrity.'
        ],['Receptor detects the stimulus.','Afferent neuron enters the CNS.','Integration occurs in CNS gray matter.','Efferent neuron activates an effector.'],'Reflex loop',[['1 Receptor','Detect'],['2 Afferent','Carry in'],['3 Integration','Process'],['4 Efferent','Carry out'],['5 Effector','Respond']],['RAIME','Receptor · Afferent · Integration · Motor · Effector','Trace the five stations in order.'],'A withdrawal reflex and the conscious perception of pain are related but not identical events.',
        'Reflex physiology is not a basis for recommending treatment; unexpected neurologic signs warrant appropriate assessment.',
        'Which neuron directly brings a command to the responding muscle?',[['Motor/efferent neuron',true,'Correct. It is the output limb of the arc.'],['Sensory/afferent neuron',false,'That carries information from the receptor.'],['Dorsal-root ganglion',false,'That is a cluster of sensory cell bodies.']]),
        M('l10-m8','ANS bridge for this quiz bank','Sympathetic mobilizes; parasympathetic supports routine restorative functions.',[
          'The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary motor branch serving smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands. Its sympathetic division generally mobilizes the body during challenge, increasing selected activities needed for action; its parasympathetic division predominates during many routine conditions and supports digestion, urination and defecation.',
          'In the textbook framing, acetylcholine is released at parasympathetic nerve endings at target organs, while sympathetic end organs commonly receive norepinephrine. Both divisions use acetylcholine at their first ganglionic synapse. Lesson 11 expands this preview; it appears here because the supplied Lesson 10 quiz tests it directly.'
        ],['ANS is involuntary motor output.','Sympathetic prepares for challenge.','Parasympathetic supports rest-and-digest functions.','ACh is used at parasympathetic target endings.','Both divisions use ACh before the ganglion.'],'Autonomic balance',[['Sympathetic','Mobilize'],['Parasympathetic','Restore and maintain'],['Shared first synapse','ACh'],['Parasympathetic target','ACh'],['Common sympathetic target','Norepinephrine']],['S = stress; P = peace','Sympathetic mobilizes, parasympathetic maintains','Use the pattern, not the idea that one division is always on alone.'],'The two divisions often have opposing effects, yet both continuously contribute to organ regulation.',
        'Digestive symptoms are not proof of a particular autonomic disorder; anatomy helps frame questions, not self-diagnose.',
        'Which transmitter does the parasympathetic division release at its target endings in this course?',[['Acetylcholine',true,'Correct. This is the quiz-bank wording and textbook pattern.'],['Dopamine',false,'Dopamine has other CNS roles.'],['Epinephrine only',false,'That is not the standard parasympathetic target transmitter.']])
      ]}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'A hot surface and a fast response',scenario:'A person touches a hot tray, withdraws the hand, then notices pain and a racing heart.',prompts:['Trace the withdrawal reflex from receptor to effector.','Separate the spinal reflex from conscious perception.','Name the autonomic division that supports the challenge response.','Explain why this sequence does not identify the cause of persistent symptoms.'],takeaway:'Map sensory, integrative, motor and autonomic events as connected layers, while leaving diagnosis to clinical assessment.'},
    recap:['CNS means brain and spinal cord; PNS means nerves and ganglia.','Afferent arrives at the CNS; efferent exits.','Glia support, defend and myelinate neural tissue.','Action potentials depend on ion movement; neurotransmitters carry signals across chemical synapses.','A tract is a CNS axon bundle; a nerve is a PNS bundle.','Dorsal roots are sensory, ventral roots motor, and spinal nerves mixed.','Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions shift involuntary organ activity.'],
    flashcards:[{front:'Afferent neuron',back:'Carries sensory information toward the CNS.',tag:'organization'},{front:'Efferent neuron',back:'Carries motor output away from the CNS.',tag:'organization'},{front:'Unipolar neuron',back:'Common structural type for general sensory neurons.',tag:'neuron'},{front:'Astrocyte',back:'Star-shaped CNS glial cell that supports and regulates the neural environment.',tag:'glia'},{front:'Schwann cell',back:'Cell that forms PNS myelin.',tag:'glia'},{front:'Neurotransmitter',back:'Chemical messenger released across a synapse.',tag:'signal'},{front:'Tract',back:'Bundle of axons within the CNS.',tag:'location'},{front:'Ganglion',back:'Cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS.',tag:'location'},{front:'Gray matter',back:'Region rich in neuron somas, dendrites and synapses.',tag:'location'},{front:'Dorsal root',back:'Sensory/afferent root of a spinal nerve.',tag:'spinal cord'},{front:'Sympathetic',back:'Autonomic division that mobilizes body functions during challenge.',tag:'ANS'},{front:'Parasympathetic',back:'Autonomic division supporting routine restorative and digestive functions.',tag:'ANS'},{front:'Depressant',back:'Drug category that reduces aspects of CNS activity; Valium is the quiz example.',tag:'course vocabulary'},{front:'Anabolic steroid',back:'Substance class with androgen-like effects; not a stimulant/depressant/hallucinogen category.',tag:'course vocabulary'}],
    practice:[{prompt:'Contrast CNS and PNS.',answer:'CNS is brain and spinal cord; PNS is neural tissue outside them, including nerves and ganglia.',rationale:'Location defines the two divisions.',tag:'organization'},{prompt:'What direction does an afferent neuron carry information?',answer:'Toward the CNS.',rationale:'Afferent arrives.',tag:'organization'},{prompt:'Match CNS and PNS myelin cells.',answer:'Oligodendrocytes in CNS; Schwann cells in PNS.',rationale:'The location determines the glial name.',tag:'glia'},{prompt:'Why does myelin speed conduction?',answer:'It concentrates membrane exchange at nodes so the impulse propagates saltatorily.',rationale:'Less membrane must depolarize continuously.',tag:'signal'},{prompt:'Distinguish tract, nerve, nucleus and ganglion.',answer:'Tract = CNS axons; nerve = PNS axons; nucleus = CNS somas; ganglion = PNS somas.',rationale:'Sort by material and location.',tag:'location'},{prompt:'Where does the spinal cord begin superiorly?',answer:'As a continuation of the medulla oblongata.',rationale:'This is the brainstem-to-cord transition.',tag:'spinal cord'},{prompt:'Why are all spinal nerves mixed?',answer:'They contain sensory fibers from dorsal roots and motor fibers from ventral roots.',rationale:'The roots unite before the nerve continues.',tag:'spinal cord'},{prompt:'List a five-part reflex arc.',answer:'Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector.',rationale:'This traces stimulus to response.',tag:'reflex'},{prompt:'Which ANS division supports digestion and urination?',answer:'Parasympathetic.',rationale:'It supports many routine vegetative activities.',tag:'ANS'},{prompt:'Which transmitter is released at parasympathetic target endings in this course?',answer:'Acetylcholine.',rationale:'This is the textbook and quiz-bank pattern.',tag:'ANS'}]
  };
})();
